WEBVTT

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:03.697
Friday, five in the afternoon.

00:00:03.997 --> 00:00:08.992
Someone on the product team opens the settings panel and edits the ticket classifier

00:00:08.992 --> 00:00:09.408
prompt.

00:00:10.058 --> 00:00:15.233
They swap the word urgent for high priority, to sound a bit more polite.

00:00:15.533 --> 00:00:17.051
Nobody runs a test.

00:00:17.351 --> 00:00:19.599
After all, it's just text.

00:00:20.249 --> 00:00:22.733
On Monday, the urgent queue is empty.

00:00:23.033 --> 00:00:27.370
The code that read the model's output was looking for the word urgent.

00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:31.958
Half of the critical tickets spent the whole weekend in the regular queue.

00:00:32.608 --> 00:00:34.621
The prompt was an interface.

00:00:34.921 --> 00:00:36.719
Nobody treated it like one.

00:00:37.019 --> 00:00:41.092
In this lesson, you'll see what it means to treat it that way, and the four

00:00:41.092 --> 00:00:44.242
questions that turn a prompt from a draft into a contract.

00:00:45.442 --> 00:00:49.903
A production prompt is an API contract, between your code and the model.

00:00:50.203 --> 00:00:55.336
It has the same parts as any interface you already version and test.

00:00:55.636 --> 00:01:01.046
You'd never let someone rename a field in a public API from a settings panel on a

00:01:01.046 --> 00:01:02.181
Friday afternoon.

00:01:02.481 --> 00:01:04.715
The prompt deserves the same respect.

00:01:05.915 --> 00:01:07.383
Let's go through the contract.

00:01:07.683 --> 00:01:12.450
The calling code knows exactly what it sends, and what it expects back.

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:15.455
The input has a defined shape.

00:01:15.755 --> 00:01:19.340
Which variables, what types, and where they come from.

00:01:19.990 --> 00:01:23.426
The prompt has a name, a version and an owner.

00:01:23.726 --> 00:01:26.624
Changing the prompt is changing the interface.

00:01:27.274 --> 00:01:32.248
The output follows a schema, with fixed fields, not free text that someone has to

00:01:32.248 --> 00:01:32.862
interpret.

00:01:33.512 --> 00:01:39.794
The code validates that output before using it, just like it would with any external

00:01:39.794 --> 00:01:40.766
API response.

00:01:41.416 --> 00:01:46.534
And a set of test cases, the eval, runs against every new version of the prompt.

00:01:47.184 --> 00:01:51.172
That Friday afternoon broke steps four and six at the same time.

00:01:51.472 --> 00:01:54.865
The word in the output was part of the contract.

00:01:55.165 --> 00:01:58.411
And nobody had a test that failed when it changed.

00:01:59.611 --> 00:02:03.174
Before any prompt goes to production, answer four questions.

00:02:03.824 --> 00:02:04.313
Input.

00:02:04.613 --> 00:02:06.700
What goes in, and from where?

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:12.640
For the classifier: the ticket title and body, as plain text, with personal data

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:13.768
already removed.

00:02:14.418 --> 00:02:14.960
Output.

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:16.604
What format comes out?

00:02:16.904 --> 00:02:22.266
A priority field that can only be urgent or normal, and a reason field with a short

00:02:22.266 --> 00:02:23.042
explanation.

00:02:23.692 --> 00:02:24.481
Version.

00:02:24.781 --> 00:02:26.345
How do I know which one ran?

00:02:26.645 --> 00:02:30.794
Ticket classifier, version seven, recorded on every trace.

00:02:31.444 --> 00:02:32.043
Test.

00:02:32.343 --> 00:02:34.142
How do I know it didn't get worse?

00:02:34.442 --> 00:02:37.594
Two hundred labeled tickets, run on every change.

00:02:38.244 --> 00:02:42.556
If any answer is I don't know, the prompt is still a draft.

00:02:42.856 --> 00:02:45.043
Even if it's already in production.

00:02:45.693 --> 00:02:49.440
And on input, also write down what must not go in.

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:56.396
Support tickets at a Brazilian bank often carry a national ID number, a payment key,

00:02:56.396 --> 00:02:58.218
or pieces of a receipt.

00:02:58.518 --> 00:03:02.297
That's personal data under Brazil's data protection law.

00:03:02.597 --> 00:03:06.051
The contract says your code masks it before the call.

00:03:06.351 --> 00:03:11.704
And the eval has a case proving sensitive data doesn't leak to the provider.

00:03:12.354 --> 00:03:14.601
That goes for the test cases too.

00:03:14.901 --> 00:03:17.829
A private repository doesn't cancel the law.

00:03:18.129 --> 00:03:24.454
If a real ID number sits in a test case, it goes to the provider on every eval run.

00:03:24.754 --> 00:03:26.472
Mask the fixture as well.

00:03:27.672 --> 00:03:29.117
Here's one for you.

00:03:29.417 --> 00:03:31.902
On Monday, the urgent queue is empty.

00:03:32.202 --> 00:03:35.902
Friday's trace says ticket classifier, version seven.

00:03:36.202 --> 00:03:38.937
But the settings panel doesn't record any version.

00:03:39.237 --> 00:03:40.939
What does that already tell you?

00:03:43.989 --> 00:03:46.510
It tells you the change went around the contract.

00:03:46.810 --> 00:03:51.759
The trace says version seven, but the text that actually ran isn't version seven

00:03:51.759 --> 00:03:52.997
from the repository.

00:03:53.297 --> 00:03:55.964
You can't match what ran to the versioned file.

00:03:56.264 --> 00:03:58.539
So the trace can't help you investigate.

00:03:59.189 --> 00:04:01.657
And an eval alone wouldn't save you either.

00:04:01.957 --> 00:04:06.684
It would test the file in the repository, while production runs something else.

00:04:06.984 --> 00:04:12.175
The next step is to take the prompt out of the panel, version the file, and reject

00:04:12.175 --> 00:04:14.391
any output the schema doesn't know.

00:04:15.591 --> 00:04:18.739
The most common trap is exactly that settings panel.

00:04:19.039 --> 00:04:22.787
It looks harmless, because the text is right there, visible.

00:04:23.087 --> 00:04:27.983
In practice, it's a deploy with no diff, no review, and no eval.

00:04:28.633 --> 00:04:32.682
And the person who edited it on Friday wasn't being reckless.

00:04:32.982 --> 00:04:36.103
The path the product offered was the wrong one.

00:04:36.403 --> 00:04:38.449
Fix the path, not the person.

00:04:39.099 --> 00:04:40.898
The other extreme breaks too.

00:04:41.198 --> 00:04:46.354
Three copies of the same prompt, in three services, each one almost the same.

00:04:46.654 --> 00:04:49.997
A bug gets fixed in one, and the other two stay stale.

00:04:50.297 --> 00:04:53.273
That smell is called scattered prompt strings.

00:04:53.573 --> 00:04:57.659
The cure is one versioned template, reused by every service.

00:04:58.309 --> 00:05:00.333
And there's a quieter one.

00:05:00.633 --> 00:05:04.081
Say the schema asks for priority and reason.

00:05:04.381 --> 00:05:08.604
In four percent of cases, the model returns only the priority.

00:05:08.904 --> 00:05:13.174
And the code quietly fills in a default reason and moves on.

00:05:13.474 --> 00:05:17.553
That's the same fragility as searching the text for the word urgent.

00:05:17.853 --> 00:05:23.282
Incomplete output should fail validation, and go to a retry or a review queue.

00:05:23.582 --> 00:05:26.960
If the field is really optional, say so in the schema.

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:33.087
Now take one of your own prompts and write its contract, in five minutes, in a file

00:05:33.087 --> 00:05:34.571
right next to the prompt.

00:05:34.871 --> 00:05:37.194
A name, a version, an owner.

00:05:37.844 --> 00:05:40.155
What goes in, and what must stay out.

00:05:40.805 --> 00:05:46.075
If you couldn't fill in the output without hesitating, your code probably parses

00:05:46.075 --> 00:05:46.734
free text.

00:05:47.034 --> 00:05:49.384
That's a smell called free text handoff.

00:05:50.034 --> 00:05:54.356
And if there's nothing to put next to eval, that's untested prompt.

00:05:55.006 --> 00:05:58.801
Then compare the contract with the code that consumes the output.

00:05:59.101 --> 00:06:04.415
If the code looks for a Portuguese word, and the contract promises an English value,

00:06:04.415 --> 00:06:07.325
you just found Friday's bug before it happens.

00:06:07.625 --> 00:06:10.454
And the fix isn't translating the schema.

00:06:10.754 --> 00:06:15.030
It's reading only the validated field, and deleting the word search.

00:06:16.230 --> 00:06:17.814
So what changes in practice?

00:06:18.114 --> 00:06:22.015
The prompt lives in the repository, in its own file.

00:06:22.315 --> 00:06:24.755
Not scattered as strings across the code.

00:06:25.405 --> 00:06:29.190
Prompt changes go through review, like any other code change.

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:32.995
Output is never read by searching the text for a word.

00:06:33.295 --> 00:06:34.729
It's read from a field.

00:06:35.379 --> 00:06:40.533
And every call records the prompt version on the trace, so you know what ran when

00:06:40.533 --> 00:06:41.869
something goes wrong.

00:06:42.519 --> 00:06:45.116
And a small change still follows the ritual.

00:06:45.416 --> 00:06:49.749
Say product wants a summary of five sentences instead of three.

00:06:50.049 --> 00:06:55.113
You bump the version, run the eval, and ship only if the length check still passes.

00:06:55.413 --> 00:06:58.238
The goal isn't a prompt that never changes.

00:06:58.538 --> 00:07:02.316
It's a prompt you can change with a test, and a way back.

00:07:03.516 --> 00:07:05.502
So here's the idea to take with you.

00:07:05.802 --> 00:07:10.079
Treating the prompt as an interface doesn't make the model deterministic.

00:07:10.379 --> 00:07:13.731
It makes the rest of your system ready for when it isn't.

00:07:14.031 --> 00:07:17.213
And it means your team stops being afraid to touch it.

00:07:17.863 --> 00:07:22.445
In the next lesson, we'll see what happens when that interface grows and nobody

00:07:22.445 --> 00:07:26.215
prunes it: prompt bloat, where every incident left a rule behind.

00:07:26.515 --> 00:07:27.345
See you there.
